ightforward: Firstly, the roots\n\nof 0 can be computed via Theorem 4. Secondly, any nonzero idempotent x has\n\na unique j th root, namely x itself (as all y∈M n are idempotent or nilpotent,\n\nshowing uniqueness is trivial). Thirdly, the roots of any nonzero nilpotent x\n\ncan be found from the theorem that follows (the theorem actually holds for\n\nany x∈M n, as long as x 6=0). As it turns out, if a root y of a nonzero x does\n\nexist, it is unique.\n\nTheorem 6 Let j∈N and let x=h d, k, m i ∈ M n\\ {0}. Then a j th root of x exists\n\nin M n iﬀ d is an integer multiple of j. In this case, the j th root is unique in M n and\n\ngiven by y=h d 0, k 0, m 0 i ∈ M n\\ {0}, where\n\nd 0=d\n\nj, k 0=k+ (j−1) min(0, d 0), m 0=m+ (j−1) max(0, d 0).(28)\n\nProof We take d≥0. Then (17) and x=h d, k, m i ∈ M n\\ {0}yield\n\n1≤k≤m≤n−d. (29)\n\nWe seek y∈M n such that x=y j. Since y 6=0, we can set y=h d 0, k 0, m 0 i ∈ M n\\{0}.\n\nBy Corollary 2,y exists iﬀ there exist integers d 0,k 0, and m 0 satisfying\n\nd=jd 0, k =k 0, m =m 0−(j−1)d 0,(30)\n\nwhich, because of (17), must also satisfy\n\n1≤k 0≤m 0≤n−d 0.(31)\n\nIn (30) and (31), we took d 0≥0 because no negative d 0 can satisfy the ﬁrst equality\n\n(30). If d is not an integer multiple of j, then the ﬁrst equality (30) cannot be satisﬁed,\n\nso no root y exists. If d is an integer multiple of j, we can uniquely solve (30) for d 0,\n\nk 0,m 0; the solution thus obtained is given by (28) (when (28) is specialized to d > 0).\n\nUsing (29), it is then easy to show that the aforementioned d 0,k 0,m 0 further satisfy\n\n(31). Accordingly, in this case there is a unique root y, whose triplet parameters are\n\ngiven by (28). The proof for d < 0 is similar. \n\nRemark 2 For d= 0, Theorem 6 recovers the above-stated result for nonzero idempo-\n\ntents. For nonzero nilpotents and for j≥2, the theorem and (18) (or, alternatively,\n\nthe inequality `≤n of Theorem 4) imply that a j th root can exist only if j≤n−1.\n\nFor example, 0 is the only nilpotent of M 2 that possesses a square root in M 2(in\n\nfact, 0 has three square roots in M 2, see Proposition 8). Needless to say, Theorem 6\n\ndoes not pertain to roots in R n\\M n, such as the additional square root of 1∈M 2\n\nthat we noted in Example 1.\n\nSpringer Nature 2021 L\n\nA\n\nT\n\nE\n\nX template\n\n\n1 0(to which we assigned no symbol), so that |M 2|=\n\n6 and |S 2|= 5, as given by (32).\n\nThe semigroup S 2 has in fact been well studied Howie (1995); Ciric and\n\nBogdanovic (2000):\n\nProposition 8 The Cayley table of S 2 is\n\n0 a b e f\n\n0 0 0 0 0 0\n\na 0 0 e 0 a\n\nb 0 f 0 b 0\n\ne 0 a 0 e 0\n\nf 0 0 b 0 f\n\nand S 2 is isomorphic to B 2, where B 2 is the ﬁve-element Brandt semigroup.\n\nProof For n= 2, set e=h 0,1,1 i,f=h 0,2,2 i,a=h 1,1,1 i, and b=h−1,2,2 i(the\n\nsymbols e,f,a,b are those used in Example 1). The table given above can then\n\nbe veriﬁed using Theorem 3(or by multiplying the corresponding matrices), and\n\ncoincides with the table of B 2 in p. 32 of Howie (1995). \n\nSpringer Nature 2021 L\n\nA\n\nT\n\nE\n\nX template\n\n12 A subsemigroup of the rook monoid\n\nAs seen from its Cayley table, S 2 has 3 idempotents (namely, 0,e, and f)\n\nand 3 nilpotents (0,a, and b). More generally, the number of idempotents is\n\nthe frequently-arising East (2010); Sloane (2021a) triangular number n(n+1)\n\n2:\n\nProposition 9 S n=M n\\ {1}has n(n+1)\n\n2 idempotents and n 3\n\n3−n\n\n3+ 1 nilpotents.\n\nProof Let N be the number of idempotents in S n=M n\\ {1}. Then N equals the\n\nnumber of idempotents in M n\\{0}, which is in turn equal to the number of elements\n\nh 0, k, m i. By Theorem 4, this number is\n\nN=\n\nn\n\nX\n\nk=1\n\nn\n\nX\n\nm=k\n\n1 = n(n+ 1)\n\n2,\n\na result which can also be found using elementary combinatorics. The number of\n\nnilpotents, which is |S n| − N+ 1, then follows from Proposition 7.\n\nProposition 9 tells us that R n contains more idempotents than does M n,\n\nsee Lemma 1. As for nilpotent